## Expert Summary

A white gold asscher cut engagement ring is the most demanding version of the asscher build — and the most rewarding.

The Color Amplifier means there is nowhere to hide — every color flaw is more visible in white metal than in yellow or rose gold. D-F color is required and VS1 is non-negotiable. The corner prongs must protect the 8 cropped corners.

The Rhodium Decision is the only unique trade-off in white gold. Buyers who understand it going in are never surprised.

The [Petite Solitaire at $750](https://www.bluenile.com/engagement-rings/design-your-own-ring/petite-solitaire-engagement-ring-in-14k-white-gold-item-195428?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) is the entry that lets the asscher speak without competition. The [Channel Set Baguette at $2,775](https://www.bluenile.com/engagement-rings/design-your-own-ring/channel-set-baguette-diamond-engagement-ring-in-14k-white-gold-1-2-ct-tw-item-197113?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) is the best geometric pairing in the white gold catalog. The [Gallery Collection at $12,900](https://www.bluenile.com/engagement-rings/design-your-own-ring/the-gallery-collection-emerald-three-stone-halo-diamond-engagement-ring-in-18k-white-gold-150-ct-tw-item-203786?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) is the complete statement build.

And most buyers do not need platinum to achieve this. 14K white gold, properly maintained, delivers the same visual result for less money.

> **Farzana's take:** Buyers come in wanting platinum because they heard it is better. In some ways it is. In practical ways for most buyers, 14K white gold is perfectly correct. The asscher does not know what metal it is set in. What the asscher knows is color and clarity. Buy E or F color. Buy VS1. Set it in 14K WG with V-tip corner prongs. Replate when needed. That ring will look exceptional for decades. The platinum conversation should come after you have the stone right — not before.

